INSERT INTO content.articles
  (lesson_id, title, format, body, excerpt, reading_time_minutes, word_count,
   author_id, status, published_at)
VALUES
  ('e0000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000006d',
   'Values, Types and Objects',
   'markdown',
   '# Values, Types and Objects

Ruby has one rule that explains most of its syntax: everything is an object,
and everything you write is a message sent to one. `1 + 2` is the object `1`
receiving `+`. There are no primitives sitting outside that system.

```ruby
values = [42, 3.14, "text", :symbol, true, nil, [1, 2], {a: 1}, (1..5)]

values.each do |value|
  puts format("%-12s %s", value.inspect, value.class)
end

# Even a class is an object, and nil is an instance of NilClass rather than
# an absence the language treats specially.
puts 42.class.class
puts nil.class
puts 255.to_s(2)
```

## Truthiness is a two-item list

In most languages you have to memorise which values are falsy. In Ruby there
are exactly two: `false` and `nil`. Zero is true. An empty string is true. An
empty array is true.

```ruby
[0, "", [], {}, "false", false, nil].each do |value|
  puts format("%-8s -> %s", value.inspect, value ? "truthy" : "falsy")
end

# So this is a bug, not a guard: an empty basket is truthy.
basket = []
puts "has items" if basket
puts "actually empty" if basket.empty?

# The idiom for "present and not empty":
name = ""
puts (name && !name.empty?) ? "got a name" : "no name"
```

## Variables name objects, they do not hold them

Assignment binds a name to an existing object. Two names can reach the same
object, and mutating through one is visible through the other.

```ruby
a = ["x", "y"]
b = a
b << "z"
puts a.inspect          # a changed too - one array, two names
puts a.equal?(b)        # equal? asks "the same object?"

c = a.dup               # a copy, so now there are two arrays
c << "w"
puts a.inspect
puts c.inspect

# dup is shallow: the copy has its own outer array but the same inner ones.
grid = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
copy = grid.dup
copy[0] << 99
puts grid.inspect
```

## Three kinds of equality

```ruby
puts 1 == 1.0           # == compares value, and 1 and 1.0 are the same value
puts 1.eql?(1.0)        # eql? also requires the same type
puts 1.equal?(1)        # equal? requires the same object

x = "hello"
y = "hello"
puts x == y             # same contents
puts x.equal?(y)        # different objects
puts :hello.equal?(:hello)   # symbols with the same name ARE one object
```

That last line is the reason symbols exist. A symbol is an immutable name; the
same symbol written twice is the same object, so comparing symbols is fast and
allocates nothing. Use symbols for keys and identifiers, strings for text.

## Numbers

```ruby
# Integers are arbitrary precision - they do not overflow, they just get big.
puts 2 ** 100

# Division between integers truncates. This catches everyone once.
puts 7 / 2
puts 7.0 / 2
puts 7.fdiv(2)
puts 7.divmod(2).inspect

# Floats are binary approximations, here as everywhere.
puts 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3
puts (0.1 + 0.2 - 0.3).abs < Float::EPSILON

# Rational keeps the exact value when you need it.
puts (1r / 3 + 1r / 6)
puts 1_000_000              # underscores are ignored, and aid reading
```

## What to take away

- Everything is an object; operators are method calls.
- Only `false` and `nil` are falsy - not `0`, not `""`, not `[]`.
- A variable names an object; `dup` copies one level, not the whole tree.
- `==` is value, `eql?` is value and type, `equal?` is identity.
- Integers do not overflow, and `/` between two integers truncates.
',
   'Everything is an object, only two things are falsy, and three kinds of equality.',
   3, 588,
   '55555555-5555-4555-8555-555555555555',
   'published', DATE_SUB(UTC_TIMESTAMP(3), INTERVAL 1 DAY))
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE
