INSERT INTO content.articles
  (lesson_id, title, format, body, excerpt, reading_time_minutes, word_count,
   author_id, status, published_at)
VALUES
  ('e0000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000006e',
   'Control Flow and Methods',
   'markdown',
   '# Control Flow and Methods

Ruby''s control flow reads close to English, and almost all of it produces a
value you can assign.

```ruby
scores = [92, 78, 85, 41, 67]

scores.each do |score|
  grade = if score >= 90 then "A"
          elsif score >= 80 then "B"
          elsif score >= 60 then "C"
          else "F"
          end
  puts format("%3d -> %s", score, grade)
end
```

Note that `if` returned a value. So did the whole expression. That is why Ruby
code rarely assigns the same variable in four branches.

## unless, and the modifier form

`unless` is `if not`, and reads better when the negative is the natural way to
say it. Any of these can be written after the statement they guard.

```ruby
stock = 0
puts "out of stock" unless stock.positive?

def withdraw(balance, amount)
  return "amount must be positive" unless amount.positive?
  return "insufficient funds" if amount > balance
  "withdrew #{amount}, #{balance - amount} left"
end

puts withdraw(100, 30)
puts withdraw(100, 300)
puts withdraw(100, -5)
```

Those early returns are called guard clauses. They handle the wrong cases
first and let the real work sit unindented at the bottom.

## case, which compares with ===

`case` does not use `==`. It uses `===`, which each class defines for itself:
a Range asks "do you fall inside me", a Class asks "are you an instance of me",
a Regexp asks "do you match".

```ruby
def describe(value)
  case value
  when 0 then "zero"
  when 1..9 then "single digit"
  when Integer then "a bigger integer"
  when /\\A[a-z]+\\z/ then "lowercase word"
  when String then "some other string"
  when nil then "nothing"
  else "a #{value.class}"
  end
end

[0, 5, 1000, "hello", "Hello!", nil, 3.5].each do |v|
  puts format("%-10s %s", v.inspect, describe(v))
end
```

## Loops, and why you will rarely write one

```ruby
3.times { |i| print i, " " }
puts

1.upto(3) { |i| print i, " " }
puts

(1..10).step(3) { |i| print i, " " }
puts

# while and until exist, and are the right tool when the end condition is not
# a collection running out.
countdown = 3
until countdown.zero?
  puts "t-minus #{countdown}"
  countdown -= 1
end

# loop rescues StopIteration for you, which makes it the partner of an
# external iterator.
numbers = [10, 20, 30].each
loop { puts numbers.next }
puts "done"
```

## Methods

A method returns its last expression. `return` is for leaving early.

```ruby
def area(width, height)
  width * height
end

# Default arguments, and keyword arguments that are required unless defaulted.
def introduce(name, greeting: "Hello", punctuation: "!")
  "#{greeting}, #{name}#{punctuation}"
end

puts area(3, 4)
puts introduce("Aisha")
puts introduce("Kenji", greeting: "Welcome", punctuation: ".")

# Keyword arguments are worth it the moment a call has a bare true in it:
# resize(image, 800, 600, true, false) tells the reader nothing.
def resize(width:, height:, preserve_ratio: true)
  "#{width}x#{height} ratio=#{preserve_ratio}"
end
puts resize(width: 800, height: 600, preserve_ratio: false)

# Splat collects the rest; double splat collects keywords.
def log(level, *messages, **context)
  "[#{level}] #{messages.join('' '')} #{context.inspect}"
end
puts log(:warn, "disk", "nearly full", device: "/dev/sda", pct: 91)
```

## The naming conventions carry meaning

Two suffixes are conventions the whole ecosystem follows.

```ruby
words = ["b", "a", "c"]

sorted = words.sort      # returns a new array
puts sorted.inspect
puts words.inspect       # unchanged

words.sort!              # ! means "this one mutates the receiver"
puts words.inspect

puts words.empty?        # ? means "this returns true or false"
puts words.frozen?

# A one-line method can be written with =, which reads well for short ones.
def double(n) = n * 2
puts double(21)
```

The `!` does not mean "dangerous" on its own - it means "the surprising one of
a pair". `sort!` exists because `sort` exists.

## What to take away

- `if`, `case` and `begin` are expressions; they return values.
- Guard clauses with `unless`/`if` modifiers keep the happy path unindented.
- `case` uses `===`, so ranges, classes and regexes all work as conditions.
- Keyword arguments make a call site readable; use them instead of flags.
- `?` means it answers a question, `!` means it is the mutating twin.
',
   'Conditions that return values, guard clauses, and what ? and ! mean on a method.',
   3, 679,
   '55555555-5555-4555-8555-555555555555',
   'published', DATE_SUB(UTC_TIMESTAMP(3), INTERVAL 1 DAY))
