INSERT INTO content.articles
  (lesson_id, title, format, body, excerpt, reading_time_minutes, word_count,
   author_id, status, published_at)
VALUES
  ('e0000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000006f',
   'Arrays, Hashes and Enumerable',
   'markdown',
   '# Arrays, Hashes and Enumerable

Two structures cover most of the work, and one module gives them both the same
enormous vocabulary.

```ruby
scores = [92, 78, 85, 41, 67]

puts scores.first
puts scores.last(2).inspect
puts scores[1..3].inspect
puts scores[-1]
puts scores[10].inspect       # out of range is nil, not an error
puts scores.fetch(10, 0)      # fetch lets you demand a default, or raise

puts scores.length
puts scores.sum
puts scores.minmax.inspect
puts scores.sort.inspect
puts scores.include?(41)
```

## Hashes

A hash maps keys to values and remembers insertion order. Symbol keys have
their own shorthand.

```ruby
learner = { name: "Aisha", score: 92, active: true }

puts learner[:name]
puts learner[:missing].inspect        # nil
puts learner.fetch(:missing, "n/a")   # a default you chose
puts learner.keys.inspect
puts learner.to_a.inspect

learner[:score] += 3
learner => { name:, score: }          # rightward assignment destructures
puts "#{name} now has #{score}"

# Any object can be a key.
by_range = { (0..59) => "F", (60..79) => "C", (80..100) => "A" }
puts by_range.find { |range, _| range.cover?(85) }&.last

# A default block builds missing entries, which removes the ||= dance.
tally = Hash.new { |hash, key| hash[key] = [] }
[["fruit", "apple"], ["veg", "leek"], ["fruit", "pear"]].each do |kind, item|
  tally[kind] << item
end
puts tally.inspect
```

## Enumerable is where the work happens

`map`, `select` and `reduce` replace most loops you would otherwise write.
Both Array and Hash include Enumerable, so these work on either.

```ruby
learners = [
  { name: "Aisha", score: 92, track: "backend" },
  { name: "Kenji", score: 67, track: "frontend" },
  { name: "Marta", score: 85, track: "backend" },
  { name: "Sam",   score: 41, track: "frontend" },
]

puts learners.map { |l| l[:name] }.inspect
puts learners.select { |l| l[:score] >= 80 }.map { |l| l[:name] }.inspect
puts learners.reject { |l| l[:score] >= 60 }.map { |l| l[:name] }.inspect
puts learners.sum { |l| l[:score] }
puts learners.count { |l| l[:track] == "backend" }

# group_by, partition and tally answer questions that would each be a loop.
puts learners.group_by { |l| l[:track] }.transform_values { |g| g.size }.inspect
passed, failed = learners.partition { |l| l[:score] >= 60 }
puts "#{passed.size} passed, #{failed.size} failed"
puts %w[a b a c a].tally.inspect

# sort_by takes the key to sort on, rather than a comparison.
puts learners.sort_by { |l| -l[:score] }.map { |l| l[:name] }.inspect
puts learners.max_by { |l| l[:score] }[:name]
```

## reduce, and each_with_object

`reduce` carries an accumulator through the collection. `each_with_object` does
the same when the accumulator is something you mutate.

```ruby
puts [1, 2, 3, 4].reduce(:+)
puts [1, 2, 3, 4].reduce(1) { |product, n| product * n }

words = %w[apple avocado banana blueberry cherry]
by_letter = words.each_with_object(Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = [] }) do |word, acc|
  acc[word[0]] << word
end
puts by_letter.inspect

# The chain reads top to bottom, which is the point.
report = words
  .map(&:capitalize)
  .select { |w| w.length > 5 }
  .sort_by(&:length)
  .join(", ")
puts report
```

`&:capitalize` is shorthand for `{ |w| w.capitalize }`. It works whenever the
block does nothing but call one method with no arguments.

## The methods worth knowing by name

```ruby
nested = [[1, [2, 3]], [4, [5]]]
puts nested.flatten.inspect
puts [1, 2, 3].zip(%w[a b c]).inspect
puts [3, 1, 2, 1].uniq.sort.inspect
puts (1..10).each_slice(3).to_a.inspect
puts (1..5).each_cons(2).to_a.inspect
puts [1, 2, 3].flat_map { |n| [n, n * 10] }.inspect
puts (1..6).chunk_while { |a, b| b == a + 1 }.to_a.inspect

# each_with_index when you need the position, with_index(1) to start at one.
%w[gold silver bronze].each.with_index(1) { |medal, place| puts "#{place}. #{medal}" }

# all?, any?, none? and find short-circuit.
puts [1, 2, 3].all?(Integer)
puts [1, 2, 3].any? { |n| n > 2 }
puts [1, 2, 3].none?(&:negative?)
puts (1..100).find { |n| n % 7 == 0 && n % 5 == 0 }
```

## Freezing, and the copy question again

```ruby
CONFIG = { host: "localhost", port: 8080 }.freeze
begin
  CONFIG[:port] = 9090
rescue FrozenError => e
  puts "blocked: #{e.message}"
end

# freeze is shallow, so the values inside are still mutable.
NESTED = { tags: ["a"] }.freeze
NESTED[:tags] << "b"
puts NESTED.inspect
```

## What to take away

- `[]` returns nil when missing; `fetch` gives you a default or an exception.
- A Hash keeps insertion order, and `Hash.new { }` builds missing entries.
- `map`, `select` and `reduce` replace nearly every loop.
- `group_by`, `partition`, `tally` and `each_cons` are worth knowing by name.
- `freeze` protects one level only.
',
   'Arrays, hashes, and the Enumerable vocabulary that replaces the loops.',
   4, 728,
   '55555555-5555-4555-8555-555555555555',
   'published', DATE_SUB(UTC_TIMESTAMP(3), INTERVAL 1 DAY))
