  ('e0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000070',
   'Strings, Symbols and Time',
   'markdown',
   '# Strings, Symbols and Time

A Ruby string is a mutable sequence of bytes that knows its encoding. That last
part is what makes `length` mean characters rather than bytes.

```ruby
text = "Grüße, Aisha"

puts text.length          # characters
puts text.bytesize        # bytes - two of those characters need two each
puts text.encoding
puts text.chars.first(6).inspect
puts text.valid_encoding?

# Indexing is by character, and a negative index counts from the end.
puts text[0]
puts text[0, 5]
puts text[-5..]
puts text.upcase
puts text.reverse
```

## Building strings

```ruby
name = "Aisha"
score = 92

puts "Interpolated: #{name} scored #{score}"
puts ''Literal: #{name} scored #{score}''
puts "Any expression: #{score > 90 ? ''top marks'' : ''keep going''}"

# Heredocs keep the layout. The squiggly one strips the common indentation,
# which is what you want inside a method body.
report = <<~TEXT
  Name:  #{name}
  Score: #{score}
TEXT
puts report

# %w and %i build arrays of strings and symbols without the quote noise.
puts %w[red green blue].inspect
puts %i[red green blue].inspect

# format when the alignment matters. + concatenates, but << appends in place
# and so allocates nothing new.
puts format("%-10s|%5.1f|%04d", "left", 3.14159, 42)
buffer = +""
%w[a b c].each { |part| buffer << part }
puts buffer
```

## The methods you will use constantly

```ruby
raw = "  Aisha,Kenji , Marta  "

puts raw.strip.inspect
puts raw.split(",").map(&:strip).inspect
puts raw.strip.sub("Aisha", "Amara")
puts "banana".gsub("a", "o")
puts "hello world".tr("lo", "01")

puts "learning-ruby-today".split("-").map(&:capitalize).join(" ")
puts "  padded".ljust(12, ".") + "|"
puts "42".rjust(5, "0")
puts "abc".start_with?("ab")
puts "path/to/file.rb".end_with?(".rb")
puts "repeat " * 3
puts "hello"[1..3]
puts "9".to_i + 1
puts "9 lives".to_i          # stops at the first non-digit, no error
puts Integer("9")            # raises instead, which is usually what you want
```

## Regular expressions are part of the syntax

```ruby
line = "2024-05-17 ERROR disk nearly full on /dev/sda"

if line =~ /\\A(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2}) (\\w+) (.*)\\z/
  puts "year=#{$1} level=#{$4}"
end

# Named captures read better than $1 and $2, and match returns a MatchData.
pattern = /(?<date>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) (?<level>[A-Z]+) (?<message>.*)/
match = pattern.match(line)
puts match[:level]
puts match[:message]

# scan finds every match; gsub with a block rewrites each one.
puts "a1b22c333".scan(/\\d+/).inspect
puts "a1b22c333".gsub(/\\d+/) { |digits| "[#{digits.length}]" }

# match? allocates nothing and is the right choice for a yes/no question.
puts "user@example.com".match?(/\\A[^@\\s]+@[^@\\s]+\\.[a-z]{2,}\\z/i)
```

## Symbols are names, not text

```ruby
# Two identical symbols are one object; two identical strings are not.
puts :status.object_id == :status.object_id
puts "status".object_id == "status".object_id

puts :status.to_s + "!"
puts "status".to_sym.inspect

# So: symbols for keys, method names and fixed identifiers. Strings for
# anything that came from a user or is displayed to one.
settings = { theme: "dark", locale: "en-GB" }
puts settings[:theme]
puts settings.key?(:theme)
```

## Mutation, and freezing

A string literal makes a new object every time it is evaluated, and that object
can be changed underneath whoever else holds it.

```ruby
greeting = +"hello"
greeting << " world"
puts greeting

# A frozen string cannot be changed, which makes it safe to share.
FIXED = "cannot change".freeze
begin
  FIXED << "!"
rescue FrozenError => e
  puts "blocked: #{e.message}"
end

# -"text" returns a frozen, deduplicated instance, so a hot loop building the
# same key over and over allocates once rather than a million times.
puts (-"repeated").frozen?
```

## Time

```ruby
moment = Time.at(1_715_900_000).utc

puts moment
puts moment.year
puts moment.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
puts moment.strftime("%A, %-d %B %Y")

later = moment + (60 * 60 * 24 * 3)     # Time arithmetic is in seconds
puts later.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
puts ((later - moment) / 86_400).to_i

# Comparison and ranges work because Time is Comparable.
puts moment < later
puts (moment..later).cover?(moment + 3600)

# Monotonic time for measuring a duration. Wall-clock time can jump backwards
# when the machine syncs its clock; this one cannot.
started = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)
100_000.times { |i| i * 2 }
puts "took #{(Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - started).round(4)}s"
```

## What to take away

- Strings are byte sequences with an encoding; `length` counts characters.
- `<<` appends in place, `+` allocates; `-"..."` gives a shared frozen copy.
- Use `Integer("9")` when a bad value should raise rather than become zero.
- `match?` for a yes/no question, named captures when you need the pieces.
- Symbols are for names, strings are for text.
- Measure durations with `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`, not `Time.now`.
