   '# Classes, Modules and Mixins

A class collects state and the operations allowed on it. Ruby keeps instance
variables private by default, so the only way in is through methods you chose
to write.

```ruby
class Learner
  attr_reader :name, :score

  def initialize(name, score)
    @name = name
    @score = score
  end

  def passed? = score >= 60

  def to_s = "#{name} (#{score})"
end

aisha = Learner.new("Aisha", 92)
puts aisha
puts aisha.passed?
puts aisha.name

# @score is not reachable from outside, and attr_reader gave no writer.
begin
  aisha.score = 100
rescue NoMethodError => e
  puts "blocked: #{e.message.lines.first.strip}"
end
```

`attr_reader` writes the getter for you, `attr_writer` the setter, and
`attr_accessor` both. Reach for `attr_reader` first and add a writer only when
something genuinely needs to change.

## Validating in the constructor

An object that cannot be built in an invalid state never needs checking later.

```ruby
class Learner
  attr_reader :name, :score

  def initialize(name:, score:)
    raise ArgumentError, "name cannot be blank" if name.to_s.strip.empty?
    raise ArgumentError, "score must be 0..100" unless (0..100).cover?(score)

    @name = name.strip.freeze
    @score = score
  end

  # Returning a new object rather than mutating keeps the old one valid.
  def with_score(new_score) = self.class.new(name: name, score: new_score)

  def to_s = "#{name}: #{score}"
end

aisha = Learner.new(name: " Aisha ", score: 92)
puts aisha
puts aisha.with_score(95)
puts aisha                       # the original is untouched

begin
  Learner.new(name: "Kenji", score: 150)
rescue ArgumentError => e
  puts "rejected: #{e.message}"
end
```

## Struct and Data, when it is only values

If a class is nothing but a bag of values, say that instead of writing it out.
`Data` is immutable and is the better default; `Struct` allows mutation.

```ruby
Point = Data.define(:x, :y) do
  def distance_from_origin = Math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)
end

p1 = Point.new(x: 3, y: 4)
puts p1.inspect
puts p1.distance_from_origin
puts p1 == Point.new(x: 3, y: 4)     # value equality, written for you
puts p1.with(y: 0).inspect           # a copy with one field changed

begin
  p1.instance_variable_set(:@x, 99)
rescue FrozenError => e
  puts "immutable: #{e.class}"
end

Size = Struct.new(:width, :height, keyword_init: true)
box = Size.new(width: 3, height: 4)
box.width = 10
puts box.to_h.inspect
```

## Class methods, constants and self

```ruby
class Temperature
  ABSOLUTE_ZERO_C = -273.15

  attr_reader :celsius

  # A class method, usually used as a named constructor.
  def self.from_fahrenheit(degrees) = new((degrees - 32) * 5.0 / 9.0)

  def initialize(celsius)
    raise ArgumentError, "below absolute zero" if celsius < ABSOLUTE_ZERO_C
    @celsius = celsius
  end

  def to_fahrenheit = celsius * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32
  def to_s = format("%.1f C", celsius)
end

puts Temperature.new(100)
puts Temperature.from_fahrenheit(212)
puts Temperature.new(37).to_fahrenheit.round(1)
puts Temperature::ABSOLUTE_ZERO_C
```

`self.from_fahrenheit` belongs to the class, not to an instance. Named
constructors like this are how Ruby gets around having one `initialize`.

## Modules as mixins

A module is a bag of methods with no instances of its own. `include` adds them
as instance methods, and that is how Ruby shares behaviour without a parent
class.

```ruby
module Auditable
  def audit_line = "#{self.class.name}##{id} touched at #{@touched_at || ''never''}"
  def touch = (@touched_at = "2024-05-17T10:00:00Z")
end

class Invoice
  include Auditable
  attr_reader :id
  def initialize(id) = @id = id
end

invoice = Invoice.new(7)
puts invoice.audit_line
invoice.touch
puts invoice.audit_line
puts Invoice.ancestors.first(3).inspect
```

That ancestors list is the whole story: `include` inserts the module into the
lookup chain just above the class, so a method the class defines wins, and the
module''s version is the fallback.

## Comparable and Enumerable, for free

Define one method and a whole interface arrives.

```ruby
class Version
  include Comparable
  attr_reader :parts

  def initialize(text) = @parts = text.split(".").map(&:to_i)

  # Comparable needs only this, and gives you < > <= >= between clamp sort min max.
  def <=>(other) = parts <=> other.parts

  def to_s = parts.join(".")
end

versions = ["1.10.0", "1.2.0", "2.0.1"].map { |v| Version.new(v) }
puts versions.sort.map(&:to_s).inspect
puts versions.max.to_s
puts Version.new("1.5.0").between?(Version.new("1.2.0"), Version.new("2.0.0"))
puts Version.new("1.2.0") == Version.new("1.2.0")
```

Note `1.10.0` sorting above `1.2.0`. Comparing the arrays of integers gets that
right; comparing the strings would not.

## Inheritance, and enums that carry behaviour

Ruby has no enum type. A class with a fixed set of frozen instances does the
job, and unlike a symbol it can hold behaviour.

```ruby
class Status
  attr_reader :key, :label

  def initialize(key, label)
    @key = key
    @label = label
    freeze
  end

  DRAFT     = new(:draft, "Draft")
  PUBLISHED = new(:published, "Published")
  ARCHIVED  = new(:archived, "Archived")
  ALL       = [DRAFT, PUBLISHED, ARCHIVED].freeze

  def self.fetch(key)
    ALL.find { |s| s.key == key } or raise ArgumentError, "unknown status #{key}"
  end

  def visible? = self == PUBLISHED
  def to_s = label
end

puts Status::ALL.map(&:to_s).inspect
puts Status.fetch(:published).visible?
puts Status.fetch(:draft).visible?
begin
  Status.fetch(:deleted)
rescue ArgumentError => e
  puts "rejected: #{e.message}"
end
```

## What to take away

- `attr_reader` first; add a writer only when something must change.
- Validate in `initialize` so an invalid object cannot exist.
- `Data.define` for a bag of values; it is immutable and gives you equality.
- `include` puts a module in the lookup chain - check `ancestors` when unsure.
- One `<=>` buys the whole of Comparable.
