   '# Exceptions and Failing Deliberately

An exception is for the case you cannot handle where you are. The skill is
choosing which failures to catch, and letting the rest travel.

```ruby
def parse_age(text)
  Integer(text)
rescue ArgumentError, TypeError
  nil
end

puts parse_age("42").inspect
puts parse_age("not a number").inspect
puts parse_age(nil).inspect

# A method body is an implicit begin, so rescue needs no extra nesting.
```

## The hierarchy, and why you never rescue Exception

```ruby
puts ArgumentError.ancestors.take_while { |c| c != Object }.inspect

# StandardError is everything a program can reasonably expect and handle.
# Above it sit things you must not swallow: SignalException when the user
# presses Ctrl-C, NoMemoryError, SystemExit when the program is quitting.
puts NoMemoryError.ancestors.include?(StandardError)
puts SystemExit.ancestors.include?(StandardError)

# A bare rescue means StandardError, not Exception - which is the sane default.
begin
  raise "something broke"
rescue => e
  puts "#{e.class}: #{e.message}"
end
```

`rescue Exception` catches the interrupt that was trying to stop your program.
Write `rescue => e` and you get `StandardError`, which is what you meant.

## Rescue precisely

```ruby
def fetch_setting(settings, key)
  settings.fetch(key)
rescue KeyError => e
  puts "  (missing #{key}, using default)"
  "default"
end

settings = { theme: "dark" }
puts fetch_setting(settings, :theme)
puts fetch_setting(settings, :locale)

# This is the version to avoid. It catches the typo in the method name too,
# and turns a bug into a silent wrong answer.
def careless(settings, key)
  settings.fetc(key)
rescue
  "default"
end
puts careless(settings, :theme)   # "default" - and no sign anything is wrong
```

## ensure, else, and retry

```ruby
def with_connection
  puts "  open"
  yield "connection"
rescue IOError => e
  puts "  failed: #{e.message}"
  nil
else
  puts "  no exception was raised"
ensure
  puts "  close"          # runs whichever way the method leaves
end

puts with_connection { |c| "used #{c}" }.inspect
puts with_connection { raise IOError, "reset by peer" }.inspect

# retry re-runs the begin block. It needs a bound, or it is an infinite loop.
attempts = 0
begin
  attempts += 1
  raise IOError, "timeout" if attempts < 3
  puts "succeeded on attempt #{attempts}"
rescue IOError
  retry if attempts < 3
  puts "gave up"
end
```

## Your own exception types

Custom classes let a caller rescue exactly what it knows how to handle, and
carry the data needed to report the problem.

```ruby
class ValidationError < StandardError
  attr_reader :field, :value

  def initialize(field, value)
    @field = field
    @value = value
    super("#{field} is invalid: #{value.inspect}")
  end
end

class BlankError < ValidationError; end

def validate(record)
  raise BlankError.new(:name, record[:name]) if record[:name].to_s.strip.empty?
  raise ValidationError.new(:age, record[:age]) unless record[:age].is_a?(Integer)
  "ok"
end

[{ name: "Aisha", age: 30 }, { name: "", age: 30 }, { name: "Kenji", age: "x" }].each do |record|
  begin
    puts validate(record)
  rescue BlankError => e
    puts "blank field #{e.field}"
  rescue ValidationError => e
    puts "invalid: #{e.message}"
  end
end
```

Rescuing `ValidationError` catches `BlankError` too, because it is a subclass.
That is the whole reason to build a small hierarchy of your own.

## Keeping the cause when you re-raise

```ruby
class StorageError < StandardError; end

def load_record
  raise KeyError, "row 42 not found"
rescue KeyError => e
  # raise inside a rescue records the original as the cause automatically.
  raise StorageError, "could not load record"
end

begin
  load_record
rescue StorageError => e
  puts "#{e.class}: #{e.message}"
  puts "  caused by #{e.cause.class}: #{e.cause.message}"
end

# A bare raise inside a rescue re-raises the same exception untouched, which is
# how you log something and still let it travel.
def log_and_reraise
  yield
rescue => e
  puts "  logged #{e.class}"
  raise
end

begin
  log_and_reraise { raise ArgumentError, "still broken" }
rescue ArgumentError => e
  puts "reached the caller: #{e.message}"
end
```

## Exceptions are not for control flow

```ruby
numbers = ["1", "2", "oops", "4"]

# Slow and noisy: an exception per bad entry, for a case you expect.
total = numbers.sum do |text|
  begin
    Integer(text)
  rescue ArgumentError
    0
  end
end
puts total

# Say what you mean instead. Integer(..., exception: false) returns nil.
puts numbers.sum { |text| Integer(text, exception: false) || 0 }
puts numbers.filter_map { |text| Integer(text, exception: false) }.inspect
```

Expected outcomes are values. Reserve exceptions for the ones that mean the
work cannot continue here.

## What to take away

- `rescue => e` catches StandardError; never `rescue Exception`.
- Name the class you can handle; a bare `rescue` hides your own typos.
- `ensure` always runs; `retry` needs a bound.
- Subclass StandardError so callers can rescue at the granularity they need.
- `raise` inside a rescue records `cause`; a bare `raise` re-raises unchanged.
- An expected failure is a value, not an exception.
