A block is a chunk of code passed to a method. Every method can take one, and
that single idea is behind `each`, `map`, `open` and most of the standard
library.

```ruby
def three_times
  yield 1
  yield 2
  yield 3
end

three_times { |n| puts "got #{n}" }

# yield sends the block whatever you give it, and evaluates to what the block
# returned.
def transform(value)
  result = yield value
  "#{value} became #{result}"
end

puts transform(5) { |n| n * 3 }
puts transform("ruby") { |s| s.upcase }
```

## block_given?, and the two block syntaxes

```ruby
def maybe_yield(value)
  return "no block, returning #{value} unchanged" unless block_given?
  yield value
end

puts maybe_yield(10)
puts maybe_yield(10) { |n| n + 1 }

# { } binds tighter than do...end. The convention is braces for a one-liner
# that returns a value, do...end for a multi-line block run for its effect.
puts [1, 2, 3].map { |n| n * 2 }.inspect

[1, 2, 3].each do |n|
  squared = n * n
  puts "#{n} squared is #{squared}"
end
```

## The pattern blocks were made for

A method that must always clean up takes a block, does the setup and teardown
itself, and hands the middle to you.

```ruby
def with_timing(label)
  started = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)
  result = yield
  elapsed = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC) - started
  puts "#{label} took #{(elapsed * 1000).round(2)}ms"
  result
end

total = with_timing("summing") { (1..100_000).sum }
puts total

# ensure makes the teardown survive an exception in the block, which is the
# whole point: the caller cannot forget to clean up because they never had to.
def with_resource(name)
  puts "  acquire #{name}"
  yield name
ensure
  puts "  release #{name}"
end

begin
  with_resource("lock") { raise "boom" }
rescue => e
  puts "caller saw: #{e.message}"
end
```

## Capturing the block as an object

`&block` turns the block into a Proc you can store, pass on, or call later.

```ruby
def collect_handlers
  handlers = []
  handlers << ->(event) { "audit: #{event}" }
  handlers
end

def register(&block)
  # block is now an object; without the &, there is nothing to hold.
  puts block.class
  puts block.arity
  block.call("saved")
end

register { |event| puts "handled #{event}" }

# & also converts the other way. A symbol becomes a block that calls that
# method, which is why &:upcase works.
puts %w[a b c].map(&:upcase).inspect

doubler = ->(n) { n * 2 }
puts [1, 2, 3].map(&doubler).inspect
```

## Proc and lambda are the same class and behave differently

```ruby
a_lambda = lambda { |x, y| "lambda got #{x} and #{y}" }
a_proc   = proc { |x, y| "proc got #{x.inspect} and #{y.inspect}" }

puts a_lambda.class
puts a_proc.class
puts a_lambda.lambda?
puts a_proc.lambda?

# A lambda checks its arguments. A proc does not - it fills the gaps with nil.
puts a_proc.call(1)
begin
  a_lambda.call(1)
rescue ArgumentError => e
  puts "lambda refused: #{e.message}"
end
```

The second difference matters more, and catches people out: `return` inside a
lambda returns from the lambda, while `return` inside a proc returns from the
method that created it.

```ruby
def with_lambda
  checker = -> { return "returned from the lambda" }
  checker.call
  "with_lambda finished normally"
end

def with_proc
  checker = proc { return "returned from with_proc" }
  checker.call
  "this line is never reached"
end

puts with_lambda
puts with_proc
```

So: use a lambda when you want a function. Use a proc when you want something
that behaves like a block. `->(x) { }` is the short lambda syntax and is what
you will see most.

## Closures capture variables, not values

```ruby
def make_counter
  count = 0
  increment = -> { count += 1 }
  report = -> { "count is #{count}" }
  [increment, report]
end

increment, report = make_counter
3.times { increment.call }
puts report.call

# Both lambdas share the one count. Calling make_counter again makes a new one.
other_increment, other_report = make_counter
puts other_report.call

# The classic surprise: the block sees the variable as it is when it runs.
multipliers = []
[2, 3].each { |n| multipliers << ->(x) { x * n } }
puts multipliers.map { |m| m.call(10) }.inspect
```

Each `each` iteration gets a fresh `n`, so that last example does what you
expect. A `while` loop reusing one variable would not - which is a good reason
to prefer the iterator.

## Composing and specialising

```ruby
add_tax   = ->(amount) { (amount * 1.2).round(2) }
to_pounds = ->(amount) { format("£%.2f", amount) }

checkout = add_tax >> to_pounds       # >> feeds left into right
puts checkout.call(100)

reverse = to_pounds << add_tax        # << is the other direction
puts reverse.call(100)

# curry fixes arguments one at a time, turning a two-argument function into a
# family of one-argument ones.
power = ->(exponent, base) { base**exponent }
square = power.curry[2]
cube = power.curry[3]
puts [1, 2, 3, 4].map(&square).inspect
puts [1, 2, 3, 4].map(&cube).inspect

# method() gets a callable for an existing method, which composes the same way.
def shout(text) = text.upcase + "!"
puts %w[hello there].map(&method(:shout)).inspect
```

## What to take away

- `yield` runs the block; `block_given?` asks whether there is one.
- Wrap setup and teardown in a method that yields, with `ensure` for the cleanup.
- `&block` captures a block as an object; `&:sym` turns a symbol into a block.
- A lambda checks arity and returns to its caller; a proc does neither.
- Closures capture the variable, and keep it alive.
- `>>`, `<<` and `curry` compose lambdas into new ones.
