   '# Building Enumerables and Going Lazy

Enumerable is a module with about sixty methods, all built on one you supply.
Write `each`, include the module, and your own type gains the entire vocabulary.

```ruby
class Playlist
  include Enumerable

  def initialize(tracks) = @tracks = tracks

  # This is the only method Enumerable needs.
  def each(&block)
    @tracks.each(&block)
    self
  end
end

playlist = Playlist.new([
  { title: "Aria", seconds: 210 },
  { title: "Bloom", seconds: 185 },
  { title: "Cinder", seconds: 320 },
])

puts playlist.map { |t| t[:title] }.inspect
puts playlist.select { |t| t[:seconds] > 200 }.map { |t| t[:title] }.inspect
puts playlist.sum { |t| t[:seconds] }
puts playlist.min_by { |t| t[:seconds] }[:title]
puts playlist.sort_by { |t| -t[:seconds] }.first[:title]
puts playlist.each_slice(2).to_a.length
puts playlist.include?({ title: "Aria", seconds: 210 })
```

Sixty methods for the price of one. Add `<=>` and `include Comparable` on the
elements and `sort`, `min` and `max` work without a block as well.

## Returning an Enumerator when no block is given

The convention every core method follows: with a block, do the work; without
one, hand back an Enumerator so the caller can chain.

```ruby
class Countdown
  include Enumerable

  def initialize(from) = @from = from

  def each
    # This one line is what makes countdown.each.with_index work.
    return to_enum(:each) { @from } unless block_given?

    @from.downto(1) { |n| yield n }
  end
end

countdown = Countdown.new(5)
countdown.each { |n| print n, " " }
puts

enumerator = countdown.each
puts enumerator.class
puts enumerator.size            # answered by the block passed to to_enum
puts enumerator.next
puts enumerator.next
puts countdown.each.with_index(1).map { |n, i| "#{i}:#{n}" }.inspect
```

## Enumerator, and generating values on demand

`Enumerator.new` takes a block that is handed a yielder. Nothing inside runs
until a value is asked for, so an infinite sequence is perfectly safe.

```ruby
naturals = Enumerator.new do |yielder|
  n = 1
  loop { yielder << (n += 1) - 1 }
end

puts naturals.take(5).inspect
puts naturals.first(3).inspect

fibonacci = Enumerator.new do |y|
  a, b = 0, 1
  loop do
    y << a
    a, b = b, a + b
  end
end

puts fibonacci.take(10).inspect
puts fibonacci.lazy.select(&:even?).first(5).inspect

# External iteration: next pulls one value at a time and remembers where it is.
pages = fibonacci.each_entry
puts pages.next
puts pages.next
puts pages.peek
puts pages.next
```

## Lazy, and why the order of the chain matters

A normal chain builds a full intermediate array at every step. `lazy` builds
none: each element travels the whole chain before the next one starts.

```ruby
# Without lazy this never finishes: map would try to build an infinite array.
result = (1..Float::INFINITY)
  .lazy
  .map { |n| n * n }
  .select { |n| n % 3 == 0 }
  .first(4)
puts result.inspect

# The saving is visible when you count the work.
seen = 0
answer = (1..1_000_000)
  .lazy
  .map { |n| seen += 1; n * 2 }
  .select { |n| n % 7 == 0 }
  .first(3)
puts answer.inspect
puts "examined #{seen} of a million"

eager_seen = 0
(1..1_000_000).map { |n| eager_seen += 1; n * 2 }.select { |n| n % 7 == 0 }.first(3)
puts "eager examined #{eager_seen}"
```

`force` or `first(n)` ends a lazy chain. Until one of those is called, nothing
has happened at all.

```ruby
chain = (1..20).lazy.map { |n| puts "  computing #{n}"; n * 10 }.select(&:positive?)
puts "nothing has run yet"
puts chain.first(2).inspect
```

## Generators that free the memory

The real use is a source too big to hold: a file, a paginated API, a long
query. Here is the shape, with an array standing in for the remote source.

```ruby
PAGES = {
  1 => %w[alpha bravo charlie],
  2 => %w[delta echo foxtrot],
  3 => [],
}

def fetch_page(number)
  puts "  fetching page #{number}"
  PAGES.fetch(number, [])
end

def all_records
  Enumerator.new do |y|
    page = 1
    loop do
      rows = fetch_page(page)
      break if rows.empty?
      rows.each { |row| y << row }
      page += 1
    end
  end
end

# Only page one is ever fetched, because two matches were enough.
puts all_records.lazy.select { |r| r.length == 5 }.first(2).inspect
puts "---"
puts all_records.to_a.length
```

## each_with_object, and the reduce trap

```ruby
words = %w[apple avocado banana blueberry cherry]

# reduce must return the accumulator every time. Forget that and you get nil.
puts words.reduce(Hash.new(0)) { |acc, w| acc[w[0]] += 1; acc }.inspect

# each_with_object hands the accumulator back for you, so it cannot be dropped.
puts words.each_with_object(Hash.new(0)) { |w, acc| acc[w[0]] += 1 }.inspect

# Note the argument order is reversed between the two. It is the most common
# reason one of them "does not work".
puts words.each_with_object([]) { |word, acc| acc << word.length }.inspect
puts words.reduce([]) { |acc, word| acc << word.length }.inspect
```

## What to take away

- Define `each`, `include Enumerable`, and sixty methods arrive.
- Return `to_enum(:each)` when no block was given, so callers can chain.
- `Enumerator.new` produces values on demand; infinite sequences are fine.
- `lazy` passes one element through the whole chain before starting the next.
- A lazy chain does nothing until `first` or `force`.
- `each_with_object` takes `(element, accumulator)`; `reduce` takes the reverse.
