`case/in` matches structure, not just value. It destructures and binds in the
same expression, which is what makes handling nested data readable.

```ruby
def describe(value)
  case value
  in Integer => n if n.negative? then "negative integer #{n}"
  in Integer | Float => n then "number #{n}"
  in String => s then "string of #{s.length}"
  in [] then "empty array"
  in [Integer => only] then "one integer: #{only}"
  in [_, _, *rest] then "two or more, #{rest.length} after the first two"
  in { name: String => name } then "a record for #{name}"
  in nil then "nothing"
  else "something else"
  end
end

[-5, 3.5, "hello", [], [7], [1, 2, 3, 4], { name: "Aisha", age: 30 }, nil, :sym].each do |v|
  puts format("%-28s %s", v.inspect, describe(v))
end
```

Note `in { name: String => name }`: hash patterns match on the keys you name
and ignore the rest, so extra fields never break the match.

## Where it pays off: parsing a response

```ruby
responses = [
  { status: 200, body: { items: [{ id: 1, title: "Aria" }, { id: 2, title: "Bloom" }] } },
  { status: 200, body: { items: [] } },
  { status: 404, error: { message: "no such playlist" } },
  { status: 500, error: { message: "database down", retryable: true } },
]

responses.each do |response|
  message =
    case response
    in { status: 200, body: { items: [] } }
      "no results"
    in { status: 200, body: { items: [{ title: first_title }, *] } }
      "first result: #{first_title}"
    in { status: 404, error: { message: } }
      "not found: #{message}"
    in { status: Integer => code, error: { message:, retryable: true } }
      "retryable #{code}: #{message}"
    in { status: Integer => code }
      "unhandled status #{code}"
    end
  puts message
end
```

The equivalent with `dig` and `if` would be four nested nil checks. Here the
shape *is* the condition.

## Binding, pinning and finding

```ruby
config = { host: "localhost", port: 8080, tls: false }

# => binds the whole match; the bare key form binds that key''s value.
config => { host:, port: }
puts "#{host}:#{port}"

# ^ pins an existing variable, so it compares instead of rebinding.
expected_port = 8080
case config
in { port: ^expected_port } then puts "port is as expected"
else puts "unexpected port"
end

# The find pattern searches an array for a matching run.
log = ["boot", "connect", "ERROR disk full", "retry", "ok"]
case log
in [*, /\\AERROR/ => failure, *]
  puts "found: #{failure}"
end

# in as a one-line boolean, and => as a one-line assertion that raises. The
# extra brackets around the in are needed: without them the parser reads
# puts(config) and then trips over the pattern.
puts((config in { tls: false }))

# A key that is present but does not match, and a key that is absent, raise
# different errors - which is worth knowing when you are reading a backtrace.
begin
  config => { tls: true }
rescue NoMatchingPatternError => e
  puts "value did not match: #{e.class}"
end

begin
  config => { region: }
rescue NoMatchingPatternKeyError => e
  puts "key was absent: #{e.class}"
end
```

## Making your own types matchable

Any object can take part by defining `deconstruct` for array patterns and
`deconstruct_keys` for hash patterns. `Data` and `Struct` define both already.

```ruby
Point = Data.define(:x, :y)

case Point.new(x: 0, y: 5)
in { x: 0, y: } then puts "on the y axis at #{y}"
in { x:, y: 0 } then puts "on the x axis at #{x}"
in { x:, y: } then puts "at #{x},#{y}"
end

class Duration
  attr_reader :seconds
  def initialize(seconds) = @seconds = seconds

  def deconstruct = [seconds / 60, seconds % 60]
  def deconstruct_keys(keys) = { minutes: seconds / 60, seconds: seconds % 60, total: @seconds }
end

case Duration.new(185)
in [minutes, secs] then puts "#{minutes}m #{secs}s"
end

case Duration.new(45)
in { minutes: 0, seconds: } then puts "under a minute: #{seconds}s"
in { minutes: } then puts "#{minutes} minutes or more"
end
```

`deconstruct_keys` is given the keys the pattern asked for, so an expensive
field can be computed only when something matches on it.

## Modelling a domain as a set of shapes

Ruby has no sum type, but `Data` plus pattern matching gets you the same
discipline: a fixed set of shapes, and a match that fails loudly when a new one
appears.

```ruby
Pending  = Data.define(:submitted_at)
Approved = Data.define(:approver, :approved_at)
Rejected = Data.define(:reason)

def next_step(application)
  case application
  in Pending(submitted_at:)
    "waiting since #{submitted_at}"
  in Approved(approver:)
    "approved by #{approver} - send the welcome email"
  in Rejected(reason:)
    "rejected (#{reason}) - notify and archive"
  end
end

[
  Pending.new(submitted_at: "2024-05-01"),
  Approved.new(approver: "Marta", approved_at: "2024-05-03"),
  Rejected.new(reason: "incomplete"),
].each { |a| puts next_step(a) }

# Add a fourth state and forget to handle it, and this raises rather than
# quietly falling through an else branch.
Withdrawn = Data.define(:withdrawn_at)
begin
  next_step(Withdrawn.new(withdrawn_at: "2024-05-04"))
rescue NoMatchingPatternError => e
  puts "unhandled state caught at runtime: #{e.class}"
end
```

That last exception is the feature. An `else` returning `"unknown"` would have
shipped a silent bug.

## What to take away

- `case/in` matches shape and binds parts in one expression.
- Hash patterns ignore unmentioned keys; array patterns must match in full.
- `^` pins a variable to compare rather than bind; `*` matches a run.
- `=>` is a one-line destructure that raises; `(expr in pattern)` is a boolean.
- Define `deconstruct`/`deconstruct_keys` to make your own types matchable.
- A missing case raises `NoMatchingPatternError`, which beats an `else`.
