   'markdown',
   '# Arranging a Ruby Application

The goal of an arrangement is that a change stays small. That happens when the
rules of the business sit in plain objects that know nothing about the web, the
database, or the framework you happened to pick.

```ruby
# The centre: a value object with rules and no dependencies at all. No require,
# no framework, no database. It can be tested with `Money.new(-1)`.
class Money
  include Comparable
  attr_reader :pence

  def initialize(pence)
    raise ArgumentError, "money cannot be fractional" unless pence.is_a?(Integer)
    @pence = pence
    freeze
  end

  def +(other) = Money.new(pence + other.pence)
  def -(other) = Money.new(pence - other.pence)
  def *(factor) = Money.new((pence * factor).round)
  def <=>(other) = pence <=> other.pence
  def negative? = pence.negative?
  def to_s = format("£%.2f", pence / 100.0)
end

price = Money.new(1999)
puts price
puts (price * 3)
puts (price - Money.new(2500)).negative?
puts [Money.new(500), Money.new(100)].max.to_s
```

## The layers, and which way they point

Four layers, and one rule: dependencies point inwards. The entity knows
nothing; the repository knows the entity; the service knows the repository
through an interface; the edge knows the service.

```ruby
# --- Entity: rules, no infrastructure ---------------------------------------
class Order
  attr_reader :id, :lines, :status

  def initialize(id:, lines:, status: :draft)
    @id = id
    @lines = lines
    @status = status
  end

  def total_pence = lines.sum { |l| l[:pence] * l[:quantity] }
  def empty? = lines.empty?

  def submit
    raise ArgumentError, "cannot submit an empty order" if empty?
    raise ArgumentError, "already #{status}" unless status == :draft

    Order.new(id: id, lines: lines, status: :submitted)
  end
end

# --- Repository: the only thing that knows about storage --------------------
class InMemoryOrders
  def initialize = @rows = {}
  def save(order) = (@rows[order.id] = order)
  def find(id) = @rows[id] or raise KeyError, "no order #{id}"
  def count = @rows.size
end

# --- Service: the use case, given its collaborators -------------------------
class SubmitOrder
  def initialize(orders:, notifier:)
    @orders = orders
    @notifier = notifier
  end

  def call(order_id)
    submitted = @orders.find(order_id).submit
    @orders.save(submitted)
    @notifier.call("order #{order_id} submitted, #{submitted.total_pence}p")
    submitted
  end
end

orders = InMemoryOrders.new
orders.save(Order.new(id: 1, lines: [{ pence: 1999, quantity: 2 }]))

sent = []
service = SubmitOrder.new(orders: orders, notifier: ->(msg) { sent << msg })

result = service.call(1)
puts result.status
puts result.total_pence
puts sent.inspect
```

`SubmitOrder` was handed its repository and its notifier rather than reaching
for them. That is the whole of dependency injection in Ruby: keyword arguments
and a default.

## Why that arrangement is testable

The test below has no database, no mailer and no framework, because the service
never asked for any. Swapping the real repository for a fake is one argument.

```ruby
class Order
  attr_reader :id, :lines, :status
  def initialize(id:, lines:, status: :draft) = (@id, @lines, @status = id, lines, status)
  def empty? = lines.empty?
  def submit
    raise ArgumentError, "cannot submit an empty order" if empty?
    Order.new(id: id, lines: lines, status: :submitted)
  end
end

class SubmitOrder
  def initialize(orders:, notifier:) = (@orders, @notifier = orders, notifier)
  def call(id)
    submitted = @orders.find(id).submit
    @orders.save(submitted)
    @notifier.call("order #{id} submitted")
    submitted
  end
end

# A fake repository: any object answering find and save will do. Ruby needs no
# interface declaration for this - the method names are the contract.
class FakeOrders
  attr_reader :saved
  def initialize(order) = (@order, @saved = order, [])
  def find(_id) = @order
  def save(order) = @saved << order
end

def assert(label, condition)
  puts "#{condition ? ''PASS'' : ''FAIL''}  #{label}"
end

repo = FakeOrders.new(Order.new(id: 1, lines: [{ pence: 100, quantity: 1 }]))
sent = []
SubmitOrder.new(orders: repo, notifier: ->(m) { sent << m }).call(1)

assert("saves the submitted order", repo.saved.size == 1)
assert("marks it submitted", repo.saved.first.status == :submitted)
assert("notifies once", sent.size == 1)

empty_repo = FakeOrders.new(Order.new(id: 2, lines: []))
begin
  SubmitOrder.new(orders: empty_repo, notifier: ->(_) {}).call(2)
  assert("rejects an empty order", false)
rescue ArgumentError
  assert("rejects an empty order", true)
end
```

Three assertions, no setup. When a test needs a running database to check a
pricing rule, the pricing rule is in the wrong place.

## A result object instead of exceptions at the boundary

At the edge of the application, "the input was invalid" is an expected outcome.
Returning it beats raising, because the caller must then deal with it.

```ruby
Success = Data.define(:value)
Failure = Data.define(:errors)

class RegisterLearner
  EMAIL = /\\A[^@\\s]+@[^@\\s]+\\.[a-z]{2,}\\z/i

  def initialize(store:) = @store = store

  def call(email:, age:)
    errors = []
    errors << "email is invalid" unless email.to_s.match?(EMAIL)
    errors << "must be 18 or over" unless age.to_i >= 18
    errors << "email is taken" if @store.include?(email)
    return Failure.new(errors: errors) if errors.any?

    @store << email
    Success.new(value: { email: email, age: age })
  end
end

store = ["taken@example.com"]
action = RegisterLearner.new(store: store)

[
  { email: "aisha@example.com", age: 30 },
  { email: "nope", age: 12 },
  { email: "taken@example.com", age: 40 },
].each do |input|
  case action.call(**input)
  in Success(value: { email: })
    puts "201 Created #{email}"
  in Failure(errors:)
    puts "422 #{errors.join(''; '')}"
  end
end
```

The edge does one thing: turn a request into arguments, and a result into a
status code. Everything it needed to decide came back in the result.

## Namespacing, and the file layout that follows

```ruby
module Billing
  class Invoice
    attr_reader :reference
    def initialize(reference) = @reference = reference
    def to_s = "Billing invoice #{reference}"
  end

  module Reports
    class Monthly
      def to_s = "Billing monthly report"
    end
  end
end

module Support
  class Invoice
    def to_s = "a completely different Invoice"
  end
end

puts Billing::Invoice.new("INV-1")
puts Billing::Reports::Monthly.new
puts Support::Invoice.new
puts Billing::Invoice.name
```

Ruby''s convention is that `Billing::Reports::Monthly` lives in
`lib/billing/reports/monthly.rb`. Following it means anyone can find a class
from its name, and every autoloader in the ecosystem works without
configuration.

## The rules worth keeping

```ruby
# 1. A class that is hard to construct in a test is telling you something.
#    This one reaches out and grabs its collaborators:
class Bad
  def initialize = @clock = Time.now      # cannot be controlled from a test
  def stamp = "at #{@clock}"
end

# The same class, handed what it needs, with a default for convenience:
class Good
  def initialize(clock: -> { Time.now }) = @clock = clock
  def stamp = "at #{@clock.call.strftime(''%Y-%m-%d'')}"
end

fixed = -> { Time.at(1_715_900_000).utc }
puts Good.new(clock: fixed).stamp
puts Good.new(clock: fixed).stamp     # deterministic, so it can be asserted on
```

- Put the rules in plain objects, and let nothing in the centre know about the
  edge.
- Pass collaborators in as keyword arguments with sensible defaults.
- Return results at the boundary; raise inside when work cannot continue.
- Name things after the domain, not after the pattern: `SubmitOrder`, not
  `OrderServiceManagerImpl`.
- If a unit test needs a database, the logic is in the wrong layer.

## What to take away

- Dependencies point inwards: entity, repository, service, edge.
- Duck typing is your interface; any object with the right methods is a fake.
- Injected collaborators are what make a test three lines instead of thirty.
- `Data` plus pattern matching gives a clean success/failure boundary.
- Namespace by domain, and let the file path follow the constant path.
